WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.103
Quadrature replaces an integral with a computable weighted sum

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:09.862
For the square function from zero to two, the exact area is eight thirds

00:00:09.862 --> 00:00:16.033
We can use that known value to examine how a numerical approximation improves with refinement.

00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:21.419
One trapezoid joins the endpoint heights by a straight line

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:25.913
Its area is interval width times the average height, giving four

00:00:25.913 --> 00:00:33.267
The chord lies above this convex graph, so the estimate is too large for a geometrically understandable reason.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.308
Split the interval into two equal pieces

00:00:37.308 --> 00:00:42.400
The smaller trapezoids give one half plus two and a half, totaling three

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:50.233
The approximation is closer, and we can measure the remaining error rather than assuming more pieces automatically make it negligible.

00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:59.403
For this quadratic and equal subdivisions, direct summation gives the exact error four divided by three n squared

00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:02.901
Doubling the number of pieces quarters the error

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:10.333
General error bounds require assumptions such as bounded derivatives; this exact formula is specific to our example.
